+    - Easiest first: (1) minimum length, (2) one level at the confluence.
+    - 1 Length (`build`, after the separation step; `MIN_LEN` 160 blocks, was missing, the mod's `minStreamLength` is 75): a source's stretch to the first confluence where it is NOT the biggest branch (by accumulation), or to the sea, is dropped if shorter than that on the grid path (Chaikin and the meander add ~15%); a branch that is the biggest at its confluence, or one that leaves the window, stays. Repeated up to 8 times (a dropped branch can leave the next stretch a short source of its own); the `alive` pass then removes what was cut off. Measured on the synthetic lands of the tests (before -> after): sources 95 -> 18 and 45 -> 12, none under 128 blocks (before 63% and 64% were); on the real generator (seed 12345, 768 x 768 blocks) the tiny stubs next to the coast and the big rivers are gone and the long rivers stay. The stubs are the bulk (a stream starts at 15 cells of catchment, so most are 1-3 cells long before they reach a bigger river): fewer, longer tributaries is the price. Lower `MIN_LEN` to 96 for more of them (the distribution is two-humped, so 96..224 changes little).
